Seizures (Palliative Phase)
Palliative & End of Life
Involuntary rhythmic muscular jerking, Sudden loss of consciousness, Postictal confusion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-046
Reason for Visit: First presentation of an unprovoked generalized seizure requiring urgent postictal assessment and stabilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient suffered a sudden, complete loss of consciousness followed by 3 minutes of severe, full-body convulsions. The patient remained deeply confused and lethargic. Immediate after-hours assessment was mandatory to rule out ongoing status epilepticus, assess for aspiration or physical trauma sustained during the seizure, and initiate prophylactic anticonvulsant therapy to prevent a recurrent, life-threatening neurological crisis before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Spouse states: "He suddenly went completely stiff, his eyes rolled back, and he started shaking violently for several minutes. He is awake now but doesn't know who I am."
History of Present Illness: Twenty minutes prior to the call, the patient experienced a sudden, unprovoked generalized tonic-clonic convulsion explicitly involving severe, bilateral rhythmic muscular jerking. This was preceded by focal motor twitches in the right arm. There was a sudden, absolute loss of consciousness accompanied precisely by a loud, involuntary vocalization. The spouse reports forceful lateral tongue biting explicitly yielding bloody oral secretions during the generalized convulsion, as well as urinary incontinence strictly occurring directly during the active ictal phase. Currently, the patient is in a distinct postictal phase characterized by profound lethargy, severe confusion, and a slow return to baseline. The patient has a history of known metastatic intracranial malignant disease definitively acting as the focal epileptogenic focus.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound and severely disoriented post-event.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent seizures and fatal aspiration; spouse is profoundly traumatized by witnessing the event.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 96% on room air | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient is somnolent, rousable to loud voice, profoundly confused, with dried blood on the lips.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals sluggish but equal pupillary responses, generalized hypotonia, and a distinct lack of orientation to time, place, or person. No focal acute paralysis (Todd's paresis) is noted at this time. Oral exam confirms a lateral laceration on the right tongue margin. Respiratory auscultation reveals transmitted upper airway sounds but no deep basal crackles to suggest massive aspiration. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the history of involuntary rhythmic muscular jerking, the sudden loss of consciousness, and the observed postictal confusion.
Environmental Observations: The bedrails were fortunately raised; no evidence of head strike or limb trauma against hard furniture.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient was not previously prescribed any prophylactic anticonvulsants despite known brain metastases.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Seizures (Palliative Phase) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Postictal Phase)
Differentials:

Syncope (Cardiogenic or Vasovagal) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the prolonged 3-minute duration of rhythmic tonic-clonic jerking, the lateral tongue biting, and the prolonged, severe postictal confusion, which are classic for epileptic seizures rather than simple fainting.

Hypoglycemia - Ruled out/unlikely as the capillary blood glucose is 6.2 mmol/L.

Stroke (Ischemic/Hemorrhagic) - Cannot be entirely ruled out without imaging as a trigger for the seizure, but the immediate presentation is a postictal state driven by known intracranial metastases, lacking new, dense, permanent focal hemiparesis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient has suffered a severe, acute generalized tonic-clonic seizure secondary to known intracranial metastatic disease. The objective findings of profound postictal confusion, tachycardia, and oral trauma confirm the severity of the neurological event. Because the brain is now electrically unstable, the patient is at extremely high risk of rapidly slipping into fatal status epilepticus if left unmedicated. Delaying medical assessment until standard hours would subject the patient to a completely unprotected airway during subsequent seizures and horrific trauma for the caregiver. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to conduct a post-fall trauma survey, establish a baseline neurological status, and prescribe stat and continuous parenteral anticonvulsants.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous midazolam to raise the seizure threshold immediately. Prescribed a continuous subcutaneous infusion of levetiracetam or clonazepam to commence urgently via the community nursing team. Instructed the spouse on the safe administration of buccal/sublingual midazolam for any future prolonged seizures.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you witness the patient's body begin to stiffen or violently jerk (trigger), explicitly roll the patient onto their side and strictly time the event with your watch, placing nothing in their mouth (behavior). This physically protects their airway from blood and vomit, and provides the exact duration needed to know when to administer the emergency rescue medication (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the community palliative care nursing team to establish the continuous syringe driver first thing in the morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure padded bed rails are maintained.
Follow-up: GP review in 24 hours to monitor neurological baseline recovery.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If a subsequent seizure lasts longer than 5 minutes, if consecutive seizures occur without the patient waking up in between, or if the patient stops breathing completely after the jerking stops, the spouse must administer the prescribed rescue midazolam and call 000 immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The seizure is a direct physical result of the tumor irritating the brain tissue; it is not a sign of pain, and the patient is completely unaware during the event.
Clinical Course: The risk of further seizures remains permanently high. Continuous, daily anti-seizure medications are now strictly mandatory.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse: Do not attempt to restrain the patient's limbs during a seizure, and never force anything between their teeth. Focus entirely on protecting the head and rolling them to the side once the jerking slows.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: (To Spouse) The cancer in the brain caused an electrical short-circuit, resulting in the violent shaking. He did not feel it and does not remember it. The extreme sleepiness now is his brain trying to reboot. We have given a strong medication to calm the electricity and prevent another seizure tonight, and provided a rescue plan if it happens again.
Actionable Steps: Keep the rescue medication directly on the nightstand. Keep the room quiet and dimly lit to allow the brain to recover.

